From b53a26270b373b087e65cc03b3f7fd7e3803e516 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 02:19:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms, nvidia-drm: no Dolby Vision through a passive DP++
 adaptor that does not say HDMI

Hades' nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9073, made again for 580.196.07, the
driver of Maxwell, Pascal and Volta, whose GeForce cards have DisplayPort
ports that take a passive adaptor (dual-mode).

NVKMS asks the resource manager for the passive DisplayPort dual-mode
adaptor (DP++) behind the TMDS output of a DisplayPort connector
(DpyGetPassiveDpDongleType(), NV0073_CTRL_CMD_DFP_GET_DISPLAYPORT_DONGLE_INFO),
but only for the TMDS clock that it limits the display to
(nvDpyProbeMaxPixelClock()). Whether it sends HDMI there it decides by the
EDID (nvDpyIsHdmiEvo()), so a client's vendor InfoFrame, the Dolby one
included, went out through an adaptor that RM reports as a DVI one: one
that does not answer at the I2C address of the dual-mode adaptors, of which
ctrl0073dfp.h says "clients MUST avoid outputting HDMI even if capable".
i915 and amdgpu refuse their Dolby Vision tunnel through such an adaptor
(linux-9995, linux-9998).

NVKMS now keeps the adaptor on the dpy with its maximum pixel clock, and
says each change of it on the TMDS output of a DisplayPort connector in the
kernel log, with the clock that it limits the display to. The mode set of
this driver, which carries a client's vendor InfoFrame
(HadesValidateVsif(), nvHadesVsifRefusal()), refuses a Dolby Vision one
(Standard or Low-Latency, or the one of the HDMI 1.4b signalling) through
the TMDS output of a DisplayPort connector behind such an adaptor, or
behind one of a kind that this does not know, and the kernel log says why
(nvHadesDolbyRefusedByAdaptor() of nvkms-hades.h). nvidia-drm gives a
dual-mode DisplayPort connector NV_DUAL_MODE_ADAPTOR, read-only, with the
values of 620: while the display is driven through the TMDS output, 0 no
adaptor reported, 1 or 2 an HDMI adaptor of type 1 or 2, 3 one that does
not identify itself as an HDMI one, 255 where NVKMS did not say; 0
otherwise. Kodi does not try native output where it says 3 (or a value it
does not know), and says why; the refusal of NVKMS stays as a guard.

Unchanged: an HDMI connector; the DisplayPort output of a connector; a
vendor InfoFrame that is not one of Dolby Vision (Kodi's 3D one); an HDMI
adaptor, whose clock NVKMS already keeps; and the TMDS output of a
DisplayPort connector where RM reports no adaptor, the HDMI port of a
board wired to such an output through a level shifter. HDR10, HLG and
sound through any adaptor go as NVKMS sends them. A DVI adaptor kept the
display to 165 MHz before this too, and Hades sends native Dolby Vision at
3840x2160 only (297 MHz and more with the CTA timings): the refusal is now
by what the adaptor is, whatever the mode.

What changes is in the NVKMS core and in nvidia-drm and the KAPI headers,
which nvidia-legacy takes from this package; no function of the OS
interface of NVKMS is added, so the nvidia-modeset-linux.c of the .run's
kernel/ is as it was.

Tested on the host with nvkms-hades.h and nvidia-drm-connector.h as they
are: 48 checks (nvHadesVsifRefusal() with a Dolby, an HDMI 1.4b Dolby and a
3D vendor InfoFrame for each adaptor) and 9; the eight mutants of the rule
(among them the code before this change, and a rule that refuses where no
adaptor is reported) and the three of the value each fail. The four
modules build as nvidia-legacy builds them (the .run's kernel/ with this
NVKMS core, nvidia-drm and the two KAPI headers) against Linux 7.3.5 with
the image's compiler, with the same warnings as without this change and no
undefined symbol. Not tried with an adaptor on hardware.
